<h2 id="what-are-digital-credentials-really">What Are Digital Credentials, Really?</h2>

<p>Digital credentials are electronic records of an achievement. Verification features depend on how the issuer creates and maintains the credential.</p>

<p>Think of it this way:</p>

<ol>
  <li>
    <p>Your paper certificate is like a photo on your fridge—nice to look at, but it just kind of… stays there.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>A digital achievement record can describe an issuer’s award and provide a supported verification route. It is not a verified social-media account or independent identity proof.</p>
  </li>
</ol>

<p>Here’s what they usually include:</p>

<ol>
  <li>
    <p>Your name</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>What you did (like attending, volunteering, or speaking)</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Who issued it (say, your university, a conference, or an event host)</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>A unique link so anyone can verify it’s real</p>
  </li>
</ol>

<p>They come in many forms:</p>

<ol>
  <li>
    <p>Digital badges for volunteers</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Certificates for attendees</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Recognition tokens for panelists and speakers</p>
  </li>
</ol>

<p><br /></p>

